Health and safety in the Gaza Strip starts with a practical question: is the place safe to live in or to receive people today, not after a vague campaign. Rakeeza offers three paths you can book separately or together: sanitizing, insect and rodent control, and safety supplies for a worksite or a facility.

For sanitizing we name the surface and the use. A bedroom is not a clinic or a shared kitchen. We name the product, how it is mixed, and how long the room should stay empty before people return. We do not spray at random and leave. High-touch surfaces — handles, tables, bathrooms — get clearer time than high walls. At the end you get short airing instructions in plain language.

Pest control depends on the source: an open drain, food scraps, damp, or a crack in the wall. Spray alone fails if the cause stays. We close what the visit can close, and we point to a plumber or a repair if the path is wider than a treatment. In occupied homes we agree to keep children and food out of the treated room until the product's safety window has passed.

Safety setup includes extinguishers, signs, and first-aid boxes sized to the facility, with a simple explanation of where they sit and who checks them. We do not drop a device with no clear place. On renovation sites we review walking routes, cover temporary openings, and move tools off the path the household uses. That cuts falls and cuts while other Rakeeza work continues on the same site.

A useful request names the type of place, whether children or patients are there, and whether the problem is a pest or a need to sanitize after debris or repairs. Photos help, and the description of the home stays with the crew doing the work. We return with a time and a step plan, not a line that says we will sanitize everything.

We serve homes and facilities across the Gaza Strip, and we link this service to cleaning when a wash has to happen before sanitizing so dirt is not sealed under a disinfectant. The result you want is a place you can enter with confidence, knowing what was used and when it is safe again.
