        'body_en' => <<<'BODY'
Institutions and organizations in the Gaza Strip often need a site that can operate, not a single line from a service list. A temporary office, a small clinic, a distribution space, or a building brought back into use combines setup, maintenance, cleaning, and sometimes debris removal before the admin team moves in. Rakeeza puts that work behind one contact and a field supervisor who knows the contract scope.

We start from how the place will run, not only from how the room should look. How many people will use it, what the hours are, and what must be ready on day one versus what can follow. From that we sort the tasks: clearance, a handover clean, basic plumbing and power, limited finishes, and furniture or partitions if they are part of the request. Each task has a date and a handover you can check, not a loose schedule.

Field labor is supplied as crews whose scope we know. We do not promise open-ended labor with no description. Headcount, work days, and tools are written in the quote. If the need changes on site, the change goes through the supervisor before the headcount grows or a new room is opened. That protects the organization's budget and protects the crew from conflicting instructions from more than one coordinator.

On sites shared with residents or neighbors we watch the entry path, the noise, and where waste is collected so the project does not become a daily disturbance. A recurring clean can stay as its own agreement after handover, and so can light maintenance for faults that show up in the first weeks. Health and safety is reviewed when the site receives the public: a walking route, first aid, and sanitizing when it is needed.

An organization request is clearer when it names the site, the activity, and the date by which the place must be usable. A simple sketch or photos of the current state save a long meeting. We reply with phased plan and a quote tied to each phase, so the organization can approve one part and defer another.

Rakeeza uses this model across the Gaza Strip for homes when they sit inside a wider program, and for facilities that want one operator instead of a chain of contractors who do not know what the others finished. Success here is a site that opens on time, and one person you call when a fault appears.
